course: arts
lesson: 1
title: Music lessons and the recorder
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A recorder is a wooden or plastic pipe with holes; your breath splits on a sharp edge near the top, the air inside the pipe shakes, and that shaking is the sound you hear.
explain: Covering holes makes the shaking air column longer and the note lower, so every finger matters: one tiny gap and the note squeaks, like a straw that has split along the side.
explain: Picture the first recorder lesson: thirty children blow as hard as they can and the room sounds like a flock of angry geese; nothing is broken, the recorder just wants a soft, steady breath, like cooling hot soup.
explain: Schools start with the recorder because it is cheap, light and quick to learn, and it teaches breathing, fingers, reading notes and playing together, which carry over to drums, guitar or anything you pick later.
explain: People talk about it in their own words: in Ireland a "yoke" is just a thing and an "eejit" a fool, in Scotland "greetin'" is crying, in Wales "tamping" is furious, and in Britain "minging" means disgusting.
fact: Blowing too hard makes a recorder jump up to a shrill squeal; a soft, steady breath gives a clear note.
fact: Recorder players cover the holes with the soft, flat pads of their fingers, because hard fingertips leave gaps that make squeaks.
fact: The recorder is a real instrument: composers such as Bach, Handel and Vivaldi wrote music for it, and professional recorder players still perform today.
fact: Posture is the way you hold your body; slumping squashes the chest and belly, so there is less air and less control of the breath.
fact: Each child should have their own recorder and wash it in warm soapy water now and then, because a wipe on a jumper does not remove germs inside the mouthpiece.
fact: Tonguing means starting each note by touching the tongue behind the teeth, as if quietly saying "doo", so the notes come out clean and separate.
fact: Water dripping from a recorder is condensation: warm, wet breath cools inside the pipe and turns back into drops, just like a cold window steaming up; to clear the drops, cover the window (the small gap under the mouthpiece) and blow sharply.
fact: On the recorder, B, A and G are played with the left hand alone and need one, two and three fingers, so teachers start with them instead of going in alphabet order.
fact: A class of beginners sounds rough at first because everyone is learning breath, fingers and timing at once; after weeks of practice the same class can sound clear and together.
fact: An ensemble is a group playing music together, and a solo is a part played by one person alone; a solo sounds empty without the group behind it.
fact: Teachers often give a solo to the child most ready for that piece, and many swap solos around during the year.
fact: Many drummers and guitarists started on the recorder at school.
exercise: Oksana blows as hard as she can and her recorder screams. What is going on? | Too much air makes it jump to a shrill squeal | Her recorder is broken and needs replacing soon | Plastic recorders can only play screaming notes | She needs to blow even harder to push through it
exercise: Liam says the recorder is "only a toy". What is true? | It is a real instrument that famous composers wrote for | It was invented in the last century as a cheap toy for schools | Only children have ever played it in public | Real music cannot be played on anything plastic
exercise: Priya's recorder hisses because she presses with her fingertips. What should she do? | Cover the holes with the soft, flat pads of her fingers | Press much harder with the very tips of her fingers so no air escapes | Cover only half of each hole to let air out | Blow harder so the hiss is drowned out
exercise: Why does Mr Chen tell Wei to sit up straight before playing? | Slumping squashes the chest, so there is less air to play with | Teachers just want the class to look smart and tidy for the school photos | Sitting straight makes the recorder grow longer | Posture only matters for singers, not for players
exercise: Jayden wants drums, not recorder. What can recorder lessons still give him? | Breathing, finger control, reading notes and playing together | Nothing at all, since drums use none of the skills that recorder playing does | Only the ability to play the recorder and nothing else | A way to skip drum lessons later on
exercise: Ollie had a go on Amelia's recorder. What is the right fix? | Wash it in warm soapy water and keep recorders unshared | Wipe the mouthpiece on her jumper and carry on playing as before | Blow through it hard to blast the germs out | Nothing, because germs cannot live in plastic
exercise: Why does the teacher say "start every note by saying doo"? | The tongue starts each note cleanly so the notes are separate | Saying words into the pipe makes it louder | It is just a warm-up joke and has no use at all in real playing | It stops water from forming inside the recorder
exercise: Freya's recorder is "greetin'" (dripping). Why? | Warm breath cools inside it and turns back into water | The recorder has a tiny crack somewhere and is leaking water | She has blown spit into it on purpose | Plastic sweats when it gets nervous
exercise: Why does the class learn B, A and G before C? | They are the easiest notes, using only the left hand | Music always has to be learned in alphabet order, starting from A | C is a note that recorders cannot play | B, A and G are the loudest notes on a recorder
exercise: Gran says the class sounds like geese. What is most likely true? | Beginners squeak at first and groups improve with practice | The class will always sound bad, whatever they do and however long they practise | The recorders are all out of tune and broken | Only one child is squeaking and spoiling it
exercise: Ruby is furious that Seren got the solo again. What is the wisest move? | Play well and calmly ask how to earn a solo | Play her part badly so the teacher notices her | Refuse to play a note until she gets the solo | Tell everyone the solo is the only part that matters
exercise: What is an ensemble? | A group playing music together | A part played by one person alone | A sign that tells you to blow harder | A case for carrying a recorder to school
exercise: What is condensation? | Water that forms when warm, wet air cools | Air that leaks out of a finger hole you forgot to cover | A squeak made by blowing much too hard into it | The soft sound of a note starting with "doo"
exercise: How should you breathe into a recorder? | Softly and steadily, like cooling hot soup | As hard as you can, like blowing out candles | In short puffs, one quick puff for every finger | Only through your nose, keeping your lips closed
exercise: Which composers wrote music for the recorder? | Bach, Handel and Vivaldi | Nobody, it was never used in real music | Only modern pop songwriters | Only the people who make school books
test: Your mate goes, "pressing harder with me fingertips stops the squeaks, sorted." Is he right? | No, the soft pads of the fingers seal the holes better | Yes, the harder you press with your fingertips, the cleaner the note | Yes, but only on wooden recorders | No, squeaks come only from the teacher's piano
test: What would happen if a whole class practised the recorder every week for a term? | They would most likely sound much clearer and more together | They would sound exactly as rough at the end of term as on day one | The recorders would wear out and stop working | Only the loudest child would get any better
test: A cartoon shows a kid puffing a fresh breath for every note to make them separate. What should he really do? | Keep one steady breath and start each note with his tongue | Puff an even harder, fresh breath for each new note, like a train | Hold his breath between notes and gasp | Hum the notes instead of blowing at all
test: Noah in Toronto goes, "We should learn A, B, C first like the alphabet, eh?" ("eh" means "right?") What is the answer? | Teachers start with what is easiest for the fingers, so B, A, G | He's right, music must go in alphabet order | C is the first note on every instrument | The order does not matter, so teachers pick at random
test: Danylo's gran says "Bozhe" (Ukrainian for "oh my God") when she hears the class. What does it tell you? | She thinks it sounds rough, as beginners usually do | She thinks the class already plays like real professionals do | She wants Danylo to give up music at once | She cannot hear the recorders at all
test: Your little sister wipes her recorder on her sleeve and says it's "proper clean now". Which is true? | A wipe does not remove the germs inside the mouthpiece | Sleeves kill germs, so she is right and it is clean | Germs cannot get into a recorder | Only wooden recorders carry germs, so plastic ones never need washing
test: Kid in Atlanta goes, "recorder is mid, no cap" ("mid" means boring, "no cap" means honestly). What is the best reply? | Fair opinion, but its skills carry over to the drums he wants | He is right, so he should stop all music lessons for good | Recorder is the only instrument worth learning | Opinions about music are not allowed in class
test: Which is true about a solo and the group part? | Both matter, because a solo sounds empty without the group | Only the solo matters in a concert; the rest is background noise | The group part is only for children who cannot play | A solo is always played by the teacher
test: Freya's teacher tells her, "Cover the window gap and blow sharply." What is she clearing? | Drops of condensation from inside the recorder | A crack that lets the air leak out | A wrong note stuck in the pipe | Dust that blocks the finger holes
test: Wei slumps and asks what his back has to do with "a flute thing". What happens to his playing? | His breath has less room, so the notes are weaker and less steady | Nothing, because only the fingers make the sound | The recorder plays higher and sweeter when you slump in your chair | Slumping makes the recorder louder and clearer
test: Liam says, "Sure, if it's cheap plastic it can't be a real instrument, ya eejit." What is the mistake? | A cheap version of an instrument is still a real instrument | Plastic instruments are always better than wooden ones | Real instruments must be made of gold | He is right, only expensive things count as music
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a recorder emits a shrill and unintended sound when the performer exhales with excessive force. | The instrument is caused to overblow into a higher and harsher register | The instrument has sustained structural damage, such as a crack along the pipe | The instrument is designed to sound only at maximum force | The performer has failed to employ sufficient breath
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the historical standing of the recorder. | Composers including Bach, Handel and Vivaldi composed works for the instrument | The instrument was devised in the present century solely for use in schools by young pupils | The instrument has never been performed upon professionally | The instrument is classified as a toy by all authorities
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the appearance of liquid at the lower end of a recorder in use? | The condensation of warm, moist breath upon cooling within the instrument | The leakage of fluid through a fracture in the instrument | The deliberate introduction of saliva by the performer | A defect of manufacture necessitating replacement
exam: Ascertain the procedure most appropriate for the hygienic maintenance of a recorder. | Periodic washing in warm soapy water and the avoidance of shared use | Occasional wiping of the mouthpiece upon an item of clothing | Forceful exhalation through the instrument to expel germs | No procedure, since germs cannot reside upon plastic
